 What happens if a microservice pod fails due to port already in use?
-> 
This can happen if:
	• Another process inside the container is already listening on that port.
	• The application starts multiple instances unintentionally.
	• A sidecar or agent is configured to use the same port.
	What Kubernetes does:
		1. Container startup fails.
		2. Container exits with a non-zero exit code.
		3. Kubernetes restarts the container according to the pod's restart policy.
		4. The pod status becomes: 
			§ CrashLoopBackOff (after repeated failures)
			§ or Error
	
	A port conflict typically results in:
		• Container startup failure.
		• Pod entering CrashLoopBackOff.
		• Deployment showing reduced available replicas.
		• OpenShift/Kubernetes continuously attempting to recreate the container until the configuration is fixed.
	
	
